 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Current in the GaAs LED (pins 1-2) lights a silicon bilateral switch between pins 4 and 6, which then conducts in either direction like a triac. Turn-on is random-phase. The parts interface electronic controls to power triacs that switch resistive or inductive loads on 115 VAC lines."
  },
  {
   "topic": "LED drive",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Each grade is guaranteed to fire at or below its maximum IFT (30, 15, 10 or 5 mA). The operating forward current should fall between that maximum IFT and 60 mA. Trigger current rises at low temperature (about 1.23 times the 25 °C value at -40 °C) and with LED pulses shorter than 100 µs. IFT stability is excellent; the IR LED shows low degradation."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Blocking",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The output blocks 250 V peak in the MOC301xM and 400 V peak in the MOC302xM, leaking at most 100 nA at rated VDRM and 25 °C; leakage climbs steeply with temperature, to a few µA near 100 °C. Off-state dv/dt is at least 1000 V/µs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pin 5",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Pin 5 is the output switch's substrate and is not to be connected."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Transient protection",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "In noisy settings an RS-CS snubber (typically 33 Ω and 0.01 µF) goes across the power triac, and a metal oxide varistor protects both the power triac and the driver from transients above VDRM max."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Leads tolerate 260 °C for 10 s. The reflow profile peaks at 260 °C, with 42 s above 245 °C, 90 s above 180 °C and a 1.822 °C/s ramp-up lasting 33 s."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Approvals",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "UL1577 rated at 4170 VACrms for one minute; DIN EN/IEC60747-5-5 with the V option. The parts are lead-free."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Solid state relays, static AC power switches, incandescent lamp dimmers, lamp ballasts, motor control, solenoid and valve drives, industrial controls, traffic lights and vending machines."
  }
 ],
